r, the predicted maximum amount of electricity wind turbines could generate will be too low. For example, the graph shows that for the West region, the winds were <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the example?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9c3b41a9-5b78-4e1d-8c7f-007719d19a3e","content":"<p>strong enough to generate about 150 thousand more MWh of electricity from wind turbines.</p>"},{"id":"b75969ae-e231-48f4-99b2-284058311c49","content":"<p>so weak that the electricity from wind turbines was about 175 thousand MWh less than predicted.</p>"},{"id":"206bb806-826e-4f67-a10d-66eb9608ad64","content":"<p>so weak that the electricity from wind turbines was about 150 thousand MWh less than predicted.</p>"},{"id":"304f7360-41c8-4dfb-8503-beb90cf2c32d","content":"<p>strong enough to generate about 175 thousand more MWh of electricity from wind turbines.</p>"}],"keys":["9c3b41a9-5b78-4e1d-8c7f-007719d19a3e"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A&nbsp;is the best answer. The claim is that when winds are stronger than forecasted, wind turbines can generate more energy than predicted. The supporting graph shows the additional amount (above the predicted amount) that the turbines generated under those conditions, with the West generating about 150 thousand additional MWh. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t complete the example. The graph shows the additional amount of electricity that the wind turbines generated. The West bar is greater than 0, so the West generated more than the predicted amount. Choice C is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t complete the example. The graph shows the additional amount of electricity that the wind turbines generated. The West bar is greater than 0, so the West generated more than the predicted amount. Choice D is incorrect. This choice misreads the graph. The graph shows us that the West (the bar on the left) generated about 150 thousand additional MWh. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-QUA-E002","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"a50a1a17-4520-4c7a-b025-2a8be850fb12","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959368,"updateDate":1691007959368}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959368, 1691007959368),
    ($SATQ$a9390104$SATQ$, $SATQ$1d6e68b4-e317-4866-ac32-947f85936b85$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>East Australian humpback whales migrate up to 10,000 kilometers each year to reach their breeding grounds. Researchers long believed that migrating whales live only on the extra energy they stored up during the feeding season. But marine biologist Vanessa Pirotta and her team aren&rsquo;t so sure. They analyzed 20 years of observations of the migrating whales made by citizen scientists (members of the public who help collect data for scientific research). The team claims that the whales may not live only on their stored energy during migration.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support the team&rsquo;s claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8df92968-d159-4810-876a-9432b04fe7aa","content":"<p>Citizen scientists have observed many different types of marine animals feeding alongside the whales.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"840b7892-a261-4acd-80bf-6b09d90d675f","content":"<p>Citizen scientists have made many observations of the whales feeding as they migrate to their breeding grounds.</p>"},{"id":"8fc4f110-0bb7-45cd-9311-62475961fad1","content":"<p>Citizen scientists have made more observations of the whales migrating to their breeding grounds than of the whales returning to their feeding grounds.</p>"},{"id":"1d165ea5-cade-48e7-84e8-4738a6f76246","content":"<p>Citizen scientists have recently begun to observe the whales migrating to their breeding grounds earlier in the year.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["840b7892-a261-4acd-80bf-6b09d90d675f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would most directly support Pirotta and her team&rsquo;s claim that East Australian humpback whales may not live only on stored energy during migration. The text explains that it has long been thought that East Australian humpback whales store extra energy during the feeding season and then use that energy to survive while traveling to their breeding grounds. If it were true that citizen scientists have often seen the whales feeding as they migrate to the breeding grounds, that would indicate that the whales sometimes feed and take in additional energy during the journey, meaning that they may not rely only on energy they stored before migrating.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because finding that citizen scientists have observed many different types of marine animals feeding alongside the whales would have no bearing on the team&rsquo;s claim; the behavior of other animals is irrelevant, and without indicating the timing of the observed feeding, the finding wouldn&rsquo;t reveal whether the whales rely only on previously stored energy or ever take in additional energy during migration. Choice C is incorrect because finding that citizen scientists have more often observed whales on the way to their breeding grounds than returning to their feeding grounds would have no bearing on the team&rsquo;s claim. Since it would provide information about the timing of observations but not about the whales&rsquo; observed activities, the finding wouldn&rsquo;t reveal whether the whales rely only on previously stored energy or ever take in additional energy during migration. Choice D is incorrect because finding that citizen scientists have recently started seeing the whales migrate to their breeding grounds earlier in the year would have no bearing on the team&rsquo;s claim. Since it would provide information about a change in the timing of migration but not about the whales&rsquo; observed activities while migrating, the finding wouldn&rsquo;t reveal whether the whales rely only on previously stored energy or ever take in additional energy during migration.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a9390104","external_id":"1d6e68b4-e317-4866-ac32-947f85936b85","vaultid":null,"uId":"8fad5f0a-1d7c-4c8b-8a9c-7da456876c80","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>East Australian humpback whales migrate up to 10,000 kilometers each year to reach their breeding grounds. Researchers long believed that migrating whales live only on the extra energy they stored up during the feeding season. But marine biologist Vanessa Pirotta and her team aren&rsquo;t so sure. They analyzed 20 years of observations of the migrating whales made by citizen scientists (members of the public who help collect data for scientific research). The team claims that the whales may not live only on their stored energy during migration.</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support the team&rsquo;s claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8df92968-d159-4810-876a-9432b04fe7aa","content":"<p>Citizen scientists have observed many different types of marine animals feeding alongside the whales.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"840b7892-a261-4acd-80bf-6b09d90d675f","content":"<p>Citizen scientists have made many observations of the whales feeding as they migrate to their breeding grounds.</p>"},{"id":"8fc4f110-0bb7-45cd-9311-62475961fad1","content":"<p>Citizen scientists have made more observations of the whales migrating to their breeding grounds than of the whales returning to their feeding grounds.</p>"},{"id":"1d165ea5-cade-48e7-84e8-4738a6f76246","content":"<p>Citizen scientists have recently begun to observe the whales migrating to their breeding grounds earlier in the year.</p>"}],"keys":["840b7892-a261-4acd-80bf-6b09d90d675f"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would most directly support Pirotta and her team&rsquo;s claim that East Australian humpback whales may not live only on stored energy during migration. The text explains that it has long been thought that East Australian humpback whales store extra energy during the feeding season and then use that energy to survive while traveling to their breeding grounds. If it were true that citizen scientists have often seen the whales feeding as they migrate to the breeding grounds, that would indicate that the whales sometimes feed and take in additional energy during the journey, meaning that they may not rely only on energy they stored before migrating.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because finding that citizen scientists have observed many different types of marine animals feeding alongside the whales would have no bearing on the team&rsquo;s claim; the behavior of other animals is irrelevant, and without indicating the timing of the observed feeding, the finding wouldn&rsquo;t reveal whether the whales rely only on previously stored energy or ever take in additional energy during migration. Choice C is incorrect because finding that citizen scientists have more often observed whales on the way to their breeding grounds than returning to their feeding grounds would have no bearing on the team&rsquo;s claim. Since it would provide information about the timing of observations but not about the whales&rsquo; observed activities, the finding wouldn&rsquo;t reveal whether the whales rely only on previously stored energy or ever take in additional energy during migration. Choice D is incorrect because finding that citizen scientists have recently started seeing the whales migrate to their breeding grounds earlier in the year would have no bearing on the team&rsquo;s claim. Since it would provide information about a change in the timing of migration but not about the whales&rsquo; observed activities while migrating, the finding wouldn&rsquo;t reveal whether the whales rely only on previously stored energy or ever take in additional energy during migration.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323045,"updateDate":1730147323045}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323045, 1730147323045),
    ($SATQ$a95075c5$SATQ$, $SATQ$33734ff0-5f0d-4009-a4b7-85d44f4bdf8e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">Mean Ratings for Patients after 21 Days</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Measure</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Mean rating for participants aware of taking a placebo</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Mean rating for participants in the control group</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Global improvement</th><td style="text-align: center;">5.0</td><td style="text-align: center;">3.9</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Symptom severity reduction</th><td style="text-align: center;">92.00</td><td style="text-align: center;">46.00</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Quality of life improvement</th><td style="text-align: center;">11.4</td><td style="text-align: center;">5.4</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>To test whether a medication is effective, scientists compare outcomes for patients taking it and patients taking a placebo (a medically inactive substance). Patients normally aren&rsquo;t told they&rsquo;re receiving a placebo, but a research team conducted a study to see if there might be a medical benefit to telling them. The team used various measures to evaluate participants, with higher ratings indicating greater well-being in each measure. Compared to the mean ratings after 21 days for participants in the control group, the mean ratings for participants who were aware of taking a placebo <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"20cfa894-3a8e-433c-8c67-d7ff4c1a0efa","content":"<p>ranged from 5.0 to 92.00, indicating that well-being varied widely from participant to participant.</p>"},{"id":"6b450efb-bcbb-4d3f-905a-2a324a24b2ba","content":"<p>were lower for two measures, with the rating for only one measure indicating greater well-being for these participants.</p>"},{"id":"1dda48af-86a4-4c7e-9ed9-20e01c606136","content":"<p>ranged from 3.9 to 46.00, with no rating indicating greater well-being in any measure for these participants.</p>"},{"id":"20194632-dcb9-4dad-99a4-26447ab0617b","content":"<p>were higher for all three measures, indicating greater overall well-being for these participants.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["20194632-dcb9-4dad-99a4-26447ab0617b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement comparing the mean ratings for two different groups of participants in a study. The text explains that a research team evaluated the study&rsquo;s participants using various measures in order to learn whether there might be a medical benefit to telling patients they&rsquo;re receiving a placebo. The table shows the team&rsquo;s mean ratings for participants after 21 days for three of the measures: global improvement, symptom severity reduction, and quality of life improvement. According to the table, the mean ratings were higher for all three measures for participants aware of taking a placebo than for participants in the control group. Given that higher ratings indicate greater well-being, as the text states, the mean ratings in the table indicate greater overall well-being for participants aware of taking a placebo than for participants in the control group.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the table doesn&rsquo;t include data about individual participants; rather, it presents means, or mathematical averages, of ratings. For this reason, no conclusions can be drawn from data in the table about the extent to which well-being may have varied from participant to participant. Choice B is incorrect because according to the table, the mean ratings for participants aware of taking a placebo were higher for all three measures than for participants in the control group, not lower for two of the measures. Choice C is incorrect because it cites data from the table related to participants in the control group, not to participants aware of taking a placebo. Additionally, the mean ratings in the table for participants aware of taking a placebo are higher for all three measures than for participants in the control group. Given that higher ratings indicate greater well-being, as the text states, the ratings in the table for participants aware of taking a placebo indicate greater well-being for these participants in all three measures.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a95075c5","external_id":"33734ff0-5f0d-4009-a4b7-85d44f4bdf8e","vaultid":null,"uId":"51ddb1a4-54be-4feb-a3bd-4691271e77a1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">Mean Ratings for Patients after 21 Days</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Measure</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Mean rating for participants aware of taking a placebo</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Mean rating for participants in the control group</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Global improvement</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">5.0</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">3.9</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Symptom severity reduction</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">92.00</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">46.00</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Quality of life improvement</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">11.4</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">5.4</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>To test whether a medication is effective, scientists compare outcomes for patients taking it and patients taking a placebo (a medically inactive substance). Patients normally aren&rsquo;t told they&rsquo;re receiving a placebo, but a research team conducted a study to see if there might be a medical benefit to telling them. The team used various measures to evaluate participants, with higher ratings indicating greater well-being in each measure. Compared to the mean ratings after 21 days for participants in the control group, the mean ratings for participants who were aware of taking a placebo <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"20cfa894-3a8e-433c-8c67-d7ff4c1a0efa","content":"<p>ranged from 5.0 to 92.00, indicating that well-being varied widely from participant to participant.</p>"},{"id":"6b450efb-bcbb-4d3f-905a-2a324a24b2ba","content":"<p>were lower for two measures, with the rating for only one measure indicating greater well-being for these participants.</p>"},{"id":"1dda48af-86a4-4c7e-9ed9-20e01c606136","content":"<p>ranged from 3.9 to 46.00, with no rating indicating greater well-being in any measure for these participants.</p>"},{"id":"20194632-dcb9-4dad-99a4-26447ab0617b","content":"<p>were higher for all three measures, indicating greater overall well-being for these participants.</p>"}],"keys":["20194632-dcb9-4dad-99a4-26447ab0617b"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement comparing the mean ratings for two different groups of participants in a study. The text explains that a research team evaluated the study&rsquo;s participants using various measures in order to learn whether there might be a medical benefit to telling patients they&rsquo;re receiving a placebo. The table shows the team&rsquo;s mean ratings for participants after 21 days for three of the measures: global improvement, symptom severity reduction, and quality of life improvement. According to the table, the mean ratings were higher for all three measures for participants aware of taking a placebo than for participants in the control group. Given that higher ratings indicate greater well-being, as the text states, the mean ratings in the table indicate greater overall well-being for participants aware of taking a placebo than for participants in the control group.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the table doesn&rsquo;t include data about individual participants; rather, it presents means, or mathematical averages, of ratings. For this reason, no conclusions can be drawn from data in the table about the extent to which well-being may have varied from participant to participant. Choice B is incorrect because according to the table, the mean ratings for participants aware of taking a placebo were higher for all three measures than for participants in the control group, not lower for two of the measures. Choice C is incorrect because it cites data from the table related to participants in the control group, not to participants aware of taking a placebo. Additionally, the mean ratings in the table for participants aware of taking a placebo are higher for all three measures than for participants in the control group. Given that higher ratings indicate greater well-being, as the text states, the ratings in the table for participants aware of taking a placebo indicate greater well-being for these participants in all three measures.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323046,"updateDate":1730147323046}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323046, 1730147323046),
